 ## JavaScript / TypeScript
 
+> [!NOTE]
+> Express, Koa, Fastify and Hono are all detected by a single call-site
+> scan, since they share the `<router>.<verb>("/path", handler)` shape.
+> The scan records **named** handler references only. An inline callback
+> — `app.get("/x", async (c) => { ... })` — produces no node in the
+> graph, so there is nothing to tag; use `.trailmark/entrypoints.toml`
+> for those routes, or extract the callback into a named function.
+
+### Hono
+
+`app.get/post/put/patch/delete/all("/path", handler)` on a `Hono` instance. Middleware may precede the handler in the same call.
+
+```ts
+const app = new Hono();
+app.get("/health", healthHandler);
+app.post("/seal", requireAuth, sealHandler);
+```
+
+- **Scope:** function (each named handler or middleware referenced)
+- **Kind:** `api` · **Trust:** `untrusted_external` · **Asset:** `high`
+- **Pattern:** `\b[A-Za-z_$][\w$]*\s*\.\s*(?:get|post|put|patch|delete|head|options|all|use)\s*\(\s*["'`][^"'`]*["'`]\s*,` — then take every following argument that is a bare or dotted identifier.
+- **Gotcha:** Hono's idiomatic style is an inline arrow callback, which is not detectable. `app.on(method, path, handler)` takes the method as its first argument and is not matched.
+- **Source:** <https://hono.dev/docs/api/routing>
+
 ### Express
 
 `app.get/post/put/delete/patch/all/use("/path", handler)` — handler is typically the callback. `app` is any variable of `Express` type.
